---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Heading from '../components/Heading.astro';
import { getIndex, getLatest, int } from '../lib/archive';

const index = getIndex();
const latest = getLatest();

const derived = !latest?.synthetic && latest?.feed && latest.feed !== 'opra';
const sections = [
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why it exists' },
  { id: 'not', label: 'What it is not' },
  ...(derived || latest?.synthetic ? [{ id: 'data', label: 'On the current data' }] : []),
  { id: 'licensing', label: 'Data licensing' },
  { id: 'reproducibility', label: 'Reproducibility' },
  { id: 'corrections', label: 'Corrections' },
];
let n = 0;
const no = () => `5.${++n}`;
---

<Base title="About" description="What this is, and what it is not." sections={sections}>
  <div class="page">
  <Heading level={1} no="5">About</Heading>
  <p>
    Pitfield St publishes computed derivatives data and the code that produced
    it. Every trading day the pipeline ingests option chains, extracts forwards
    from put and call parity, fits arbitrage-free volatility surfaces, computes
    a full metric set, and publishes the results alongside the quality report
    describing what it had to throw away.
  </p>

  <Heading no={no()} id="why">Why it exists</Heading>
  <p>
    Institutional options data is priced as though only institutions should
    have it. A consolidated options feed runs to thousands of dollars a month
    before anyone has computed anything with it, and the licensing makes
    redistribution of what you derive its own negotiation. The people most able
    to check this kind of work, students and independent researchers and anyone
    curious and rigorous without a desk behind them, are priced out of even
    looking.
  </p>
  <div class="full callout">
    <p>
      The intention is to close that gap: to offer institutional-grade computed
      data for a fraction of what the underlying costs, and one day for free
      wherever the licensing allows it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    This archive publishes the computed layer: forwards, surfaces, greeks, the
    full metric set, and the quality report describing what was discarded.
    Everything on this site today is free to read and free to cite. Where a
    licence prevents something, the constraint is stated rather than quietly
    worked around.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is an aim, not a promise, and it is worth being exact about which. The
    archive currently runs on a derived feed because a consolidated one is not
    yet funded, and every page says so. What is already true is the part that
    matters most: the method is open, the code is public, the inputs are named,
    and anyone can reproduce the numbers or find the error in them.
  </p>

  <Heading no={no()} id="not">What it is not</Heading>
  <ul>
    <li>Not a signals service. There are no buy or sell recommendations, no alerts, no screeners.</li>
    <li>Not a claim of predictive edge. Nothing here is presented as tradeable.</li>
    <li>Not investment advice, and not a recommendation about any security.</li>
    <li>Not a real-time feed. Published values are end of day, never streaming.</li>
    <li>There is not a single backtest equity curve in the repository, and there never will be.</li>
  </ul>

  {derived && (
    <>
      <Heading no={no()} id="data">On the current data</Heading>
      <p>
        The chains behind these numbers come from Alpaca's
        <code>{latest.feed}</code> feed, which estimates a quote rather than
        reporting the OPRA consolidated best bid and offer. This archive has no
        OPRA entitlement, and rather than publish nothing it publishes this,
        labelled. Every stored row carries the feed it came from, so the record
        stays honest even if a later page forgets to mention it.
      </p>
      <div class="full stagger">
        <div>
          <h3>What that costs you</h3>
          <p>
            The levels are estimates, the spreads are not tradeable, and a
            volatility surface fitted to them should not be treated as the
            market's.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>What it does not</h3>
          <p>
            The pipeline, the parity forward extraction, the arbitrage
            conditions and the quality report are the same code that would run
            on a licensed feed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </>
  )}

  {latest?.synthetic && (
    <>
      <Heading no={no()} id="data">On the current data</Heading>
      <p>
        The pipeline, the calibration, the arbitrage checks and the statistics
        are real, and are exactly the code that will run against a licensed
        feed. The quotes they are presently run against are generated by a
        simulator in this repository, which prices chains from a known forward
        and known SVI parameters. That is what makes the calibration testable:
        the test suite can assert the pipeline recovers the parameters it was
        handed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing on this site describes an actual security today. Every page
        built on generated numbers says so at the top.
      </p>
    </>
  )}

  <Heading no={no()} id="licensing">Data licensing</Heading>
  <p>
    Market data is sourced under a single-person, non-professional,
    non-redistributing licence. Raw quotes are therefore never republished.
    Derived values, implied volatilities, greeks, SVI parameters and
    aggregates, are this project's own computations and are published with
    their method.
  </p>

  <Heading no={no()} id="reproducibility">Reproducibility</Heading>
  <p>
    The repository is public and the scheduled runs are public with it, so the
    run logs are third-party evidence that the pipeline has been operating
    unattended rather than a claim on this page. A clone plus
    <code>make demo</code> reproduces a full day's output from raw inputs,
    including this site.
  </p>
  {index && (
    <dl class="figures">
      <div><dt>Days archived</dt><dd>{int(index.n_days)}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Contract-days</dt><dd>{int(index.contract_days)}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Latest session</dt><dd>{index.latest_date}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Pipeline version</dt><dd>{index.pipeline_version}</dd></div>
    </dl>
  )}

  <Heading no={no()} id="corrections">Corrections</Heading>
  <p>
    Nothing in the archive is ever overwritten. A correction is written as a
    new row with a later snapshot timestamp, and readers default to the first
    observation of a given contract on a given day. Rows carry the version of
    the code that wrote them, so output from a bug can be identified without
    deleting the record that it happened.
  </p>
  </div>
</Base>
